  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p>At 5,007 feet, <strong>South Yuyanq\u2019 Ch\u2019ex</strong> is a summit in Alaska. The South Yuyanq\u2019 Ch\u2019ex Trail covers 11.8 miles round trip (out-and-back) with about 2,913 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a gradual walk with modest climbing.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>Most of the climbing comes early \u2014 the first half of the route does the bulk of the work before the grade eases. The steepest stretch arrives around mile 0.42, gaining roughly 1,200 feet per mile \u2014 expect a slow, deliberate pace through here. From the trailhead at about 328 feet, the route tops out near 3,163 feet.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=61.02192,-149.7378\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">61.02192, -149.7378</a> (tap for driving directions). Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Late May through October is the main season. Snow and ice linger into spring and return early in fall at this latitude and elevation; in winter this becomes a different, more serious undertaking requiring traction devices and warm layers.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include North Yuyanq\u2019 Ch\u2019ex, Ptarmigan Peak and McHugh Peak \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
